---
import ArticleClosingNote from '@/components/articles/ArticleClosingNote.astro';
import ArticleGoogleMap from '@/components/articles/ArticleGoogleMap.astro';
import ArticleImage from '@/components/ui/ArticleImage.astro';
import { articleHref } from '@/domains/articles/catalog';

import bambooGrove from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-arashiyama-bamboo-grove.webp';
import bambooForestView from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-bamboo-forest-view.webp';
import bambooGroveCanopy from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-bamboo-grove-canopy.webp';
import otagiTemple from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-otagi-nenbutsu-ji-temple.webp';
import yusaTeiWindow from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-yusa-tei-gallery-window-view.webp';
import kinkakuJi from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-kinkaku-ji-golden-pavilion.webp';
import kiyomizuSunset from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-kiyomizu-dera-wooden-terrace.webp';
import kiyomizuPagoda from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-kiyomizu-dera-three-story-pagoda.webp';
import yasakaPagoda from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-yasaka-pagoda.webp';
import historicStreet from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-historic-street.webp';
import higashiyamaRooftops from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-higashiyama-rooftops.webp';
import gionSunrise from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-gion-at-sunrise.webp';
import fushimiInari from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-fushimi-inari-torii-gates.webp';
import naraDeer from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-nara-deer-temple.webp';
import nishikiFoodStalls from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-nishiki-market-food-stalls.webp';
import nishikiStreetFood from '@/assets/articles/kyoto/japan-kyoto-nishiki-market-street-food.webp';

const prose = 'article-prose max-w-3xl text-black/80 [&_p]:mb-5 [&_p:last-child]:mb-0';
const mapLink = 'text-[#69746E] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[#4a534f]';
const threeColGrid = 'my-5 grid grid-cols-1 items-start gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3 md:my-8 md:gap-4';
const twoColGrid = 'my-5 grid grid-cols-2 items-start gap-3 md:my-8 md:gap-4';
const mapId = '1yt4Y8HLHzCnHDUs_gYwY-G_cjAb8xhw';
---

<ArticleGoogleMap
  title="Google Map of Kyoto"
  description="You'll find every place mentioned in this guide pinned on my Kyoto 3-Day Itinerary Map, including attractions, cafés, restaurants and recommended hotels, making it easy to follow the same route during your visit."
  mapId={mapId}
/>
<section id="section-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Day 1: Arashiyama & Kinkaku-ji</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">Visit Arashiyama Bamboo Grove</h4>
    <p>Before visiting Arashiyama Bamboo Grove, I expected it to be one of those places that looks better on social media than it does in real life. It turned out to be the exact opposite. Arriving around sunrise completely changed the experience. As the first light of the day filtered through the bamboo, the grove felt peaceful and almost empty — a very different atmosphere from the crowds that arrive later in the morning. If there's one piece of advice I'd give, it's to get here as early as possible.</p>
    <div class={threeColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={bambooGrove} alt="The walkway through Arashiyama Bamboo Grove in Kyoto, Japan" caption="Arashiyama Bamboo Grove at sunrise" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={bambooForestView} alt="View along the towering bamboo path in Arashiyama, Kyoto" caption="The towering bamboo path" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={bambooGroveCanopy} alt="Bamboo stalks leaning overhead to form a canopy in Arashiyama, Kyoto" caption="Looking up at the bamboo canopy" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>One thing many people forget to do is look up. As the bamboo grows taller, the stalks lean towards each other high above the path, creating a canopy that's just as impressive as the famous walkway itself. It's a small detail, but it ended up being one of the things I remember most from visiting Arashiyama.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Visit Otagi Nenbutsu-ji Temple</h4>
    <p>A short distance from the main Arashiyama area, Otagi Nenbutsu-ji was one of the most memorable temples I visited in Kyoto. Instead of grand buildings or crowded courtyards, it's best known for its hundreds of moss-covered stone statues, each carved with its own unique facial expression and personality.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={otagiTemple} alt="Moss-covered stone statues at Otagi Nenbutsu-ji temple in Arashiyama, Kyoto" caption="The expressive stone statues of Otagi Nenbutsu-ji" />
    <p>Some are smiling, others are laughing, and many are doing unexpected things that make walking around the temple surprisingly entertaining. I expected to spend just a few minutes here, but kept stopping to look at the different statues and ended up staying much longer than planned.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Take a Break at Yusa-tei Gallery</h4>
    <p>Yusa-tei is a traditional Japanese teahouse best known for its large picture window overlooking the surrounding landscape. It's become one of Arashiyama's most photographed spots, especially during autumn when the colourful leaves are reflected in the glass.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={yusaTeiWindow} alt="The large window view overlooking the landscape at Yusa-tei gallery in Arashiyama, Kyoto" caption="Yusa-tei's famous window view" imgClass="aspect-[3/2] object-cover" />
    <p>I almost missed Yusa-tei during my first visit to Arashiyama and only discovered it when I returned to the area on a rainy day. Looking back, the weather probably made the experience even better. After spending some time inside, I headed out to the terrace and sat there listening to the rain for a while. It's a simple memory, but one that has stayed with me long after the trip. If you happen to visit Kyoto on a rainy day, this is one of the places I'd recommend most.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">End the Day at Kinkaku-ji</h4>
    <p>End your first day at Kinkaku-ji, one of Kyoto's most famous temples. Better known as the Golden Pavilion, it's covered in gold leaf on its upper floors and reflects beautifully in the surrounding pond, creating one of the city's most iconic views.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={kinkakuJi} alt="The golden pavilion of Kinkaku-ji reflected in its pond in Kyoto, Japan" caption="Kinkaku-ji, the Golden Pavilion" />
    <p>It can get busy throughout the day, but it's still a place I'd recommend including on a first visit to Kyoto. The temple may be one of the city's most photographed landmarks, but seeing it in person is what makes it so memorable.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Day 2: Kiyomizu-dera, Yasaka Pagoda & Gion</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">Visit Kiyomizu-dera</h4>
    <p>One of Kyoto's most famous landmarks, Kiyomizu-dera is best known for its large wooden terrace overlooking the city. Built without using a single nail, it offers one of the most iconic views in Kyoto. The temple complex is also much larger than it looks in photos, with plenty of paths and smaller buildings to explore beyond the main hall.</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={kiyomizuSunset} alt="Kiyomizu-dera's large wooden terrace overlooking Kyoto, Japan" caption="Kiyomizu-dera's large wooden terrace" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={kiyomizuPagoda} alt="The three-story pagoda at Kiyomizu-dera in Kyoto, Japan" caption="The three-story pagoda" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>I visited shortly before sunset, hoping to catch the golden light over Kyoto, but quickly realised I could have spent much longer exploring the grounds. If I were planning this itinerary again, I'd visit first thing in the morning. Not only is it generally quieter, but you'll also have plenty of time to explore before wandering through the historic streets of Higashiyama towards Yasaka Pagoda.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Wander Through Higashiyama & Yasaka Pagoda</h4>
    <p>The historic streets of Higashiyama are one of the best places to experience Kyoto's traditional side. Lined with wooden machiya houses, small local shops and tea houses, this is the area that many people picture when they think of Kyoto. It's also home to the iconic Yasaka Pagoda, one of the city's most photographed landmarks.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={yasakaPagoda} alt="The Yasaka Pagoda rising above the historic streets of Higashiyama in Kyoto, Japan" caption="Yasaka Pagoda in Higashiyama" imgClass="aspect-[4/3] object-cover" />
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={historicStreet} alt="A traditional historic street lined with wooden buildings near Yasaka Pagoda in Kyoto, Japan" caption="Traditional streets near Yasaka Pagoda" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={higashiyamaRooftops} alt="Traditional rooftops across the Higashiyama district in Kyoto, Japan" caption="Higashiyama rooftops" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>Walking through these streets ended up being one of my favourite parts of Kyoto. More than anywhere else, this area gave me the strongest sense of what the city might have felt like in the past. Many of the houses are still private homes, so you'll notice signs asking visitors not to take photos on certain streets. It's a small thing, but respecting those requests helps preserve the atmosphere that makes this neighbourhood so special.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Spend the Evening in Gion</h4>
    <p>Known as Kyoto's most famous geisha district, Gion is one of the best places to experience the city's traditional atmosphere. With its preserved wooden buildings, lantern-lit streets and narrow side alleys, it's easy to see why this neighbourhood has become one of Kyoto's most iconic areas.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={gionSunrise} alt="The traditional streets of Gion at sunrise in Kyoto, Japan" caption="Gion at sunrise" imgClass="aspect-[5/4] object-cover" />
    <p>Rather than rushing from one landmark to the next, take your time and simply wander around. Some of my favourite moments in Gion came from exploring the quieter streets, away from the busiest areas, where the atmosphere felt even more special.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Day 3: Fushimi Inari, Nara & Nishiki Market</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">Arrive Early at Fushimi Inari</h4>
    <p>Fushimi Inari is one of Kyoto's most iconic landmarks, famous for its thousands of bright red torii gates that wind their way up the mountainside. While the entrance is often the busiest part, the further you walk, the quieter the trails become.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={fushimiInari} alt="The vermilion torii gates winding up the mountain at Fushimi Inari in Kyoto, Japan" caption="The torii gates of Fushimi Inari" imgClass="aspect-[3/2] object-cover" />
    <p>I arrived shortly after sunrise and was surprised by how many people were already there. Another thing that caught me off guard was the amount of walking involved. Many visitors turn back after the first section of torii gates, but if you continue further up the mountain, you'll be rewarded with a much more peaceful atmosphere. Wear comfortable shoes and allow yourself more time than you think you'll need.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Take a Day Trip to Nara</h4>
    <p>If you're visiting Kyoto for the first time, Nara is one of the easiest and most rewarding day trips you can make. Best known for its free-roaming deer and the impressive Todai-ji Temple, it's a completely different experience from Kyoto and well worth adding to your itinerary.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={naraDeer} alt="A deer in front of a temple in Nara Park, Japan" caption="The famous deer of Nara Park" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    <p>I mainly visited because I wanted to see the famous deer. One thing that surprised me was how different the atmosphere became the further I walked through Nara Park. While many of the deer near the entrances were surrounded by visitors, the ones deeper inside the park felt much calmer. If you're planning a visit, I'd recommend allowing at least half a day to explore at a relaxed pace. For a full route, practical tips and everything I did during my visit, read my complete <a href={articleHref('nara-day-trip-from-kyoto')} class={mapLink}>Nara Day Trip Guide</a>.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">End the Evening at Nishiki Market</h4>
    <p>Known as "Kyoto's Kitchen", Nishiki Market is a lively covered market filled with local food stalls, small family-run shops and regional specialities. It's the perfect place to try a few local snacks and experience a different side of Kyoto beyond its temples and shrines.</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={nishikiFoodStalls} alt="Food stalls lining the covered street of Nishiki Market in Kyoto, Japan" caption="The food stalls of Nishiki Market" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={nishikiStreetFood} alt="Street food on display at Nishiki Market in Kyoto, Japan" caption="Sampling the street food" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>Whether you're stopping for a quick bite or simply wandering through the covered arcade, it's a relaxed way to spend an evening in Kyoto. I found it to be the perfect place to slow down after a busy day of sightseeing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Where to Stay in Kyoto for First-Time Visitors</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If it's your first time visiting Kyoto, I'd recommend staying in Downtown Kyoto, Gion, or near Kyoto Station. Downtown offers the best balance between sightseeing, restaurants and nightlife, Gion is perfect if you're looking for a more traditional atmosphere, while Kyoto Station is the most convenient choice for day trips and travelling around Japan.</p>
    <p>Below are my favourite hotel recommendations across different budgets, each offering something a little different while being well located for exploring Kyoto.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Luxury</h4>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 marker:text-black/40">
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Hotel+The+Mitsui+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Hotel The Mitsui Kyoto</a> – Luxury spa and timeless Japanese design.</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Sowaka+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Sowaka</a> – Traditional ryokan-style luxury.</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=The+Hotel+Seiryu+Kyoto+Kiyomizu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>The Hotel Seiryu Kyoto Kiyomizu</a> – Historic setting with panoramic views.</li>
    </ul>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Mid-range</h4>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 marker:text-black/40">
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Nohga+Hotel+Kiyomizu+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Nohga Hotel Kiyomizu Kyoto</a> – Boutique design with a local feel.</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Cross+Hotel+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Cross Hotel Kyoto</a> – Stylish design and great value.</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Hotel+Granvia+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Hotel Granvia Kyoto</a> – Spacious rooms and excellent facilities.</li>
    </ul>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Budget</h4>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 marker:text-black/40">
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Sakura+Terrace+The+Gallery+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Sakura Terrace The Gallery</a> – Excellent facilities for the price.</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Piece+Hostel+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Piece Hostel Kyoto</a> – Stylish hostel with private rooms.</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://www.booking.com/searchresults.html?ss=Hotel+M%27s+Plus+Shijo+Omiya+Kyoto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Hotel M's Plus Shijo Omiya</a> – Clean, modern and affordable.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Where to Eat in Kyoto</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If you're looking for restaurant recommendations, these are some of the places I enjoyed during my time in Kyoto.</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 marker:text-black/40">
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/8vg6zrPdSAv9KRLX6?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Sushizen</a> – Sushi</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/yQbsLsVaxAanqvcg9?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>YOSAKURA Kyoto Gion</a> – Unagi (grilled eel)</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.google.com?q=Okonomiyaki%20Mr%20Young%20Men,%20%EF%BC%AD%EF%BD%92%EF%BC%8E%EF%BC%B9%EF%BD%8F%EF%BD%95%EF%BD%8E%EF%BD%87%EF%BC%AD%EF%BD%85%EF%BD%8E%20Higashidaimonjicho,%20Nakagyo%20Ward,%20Kyoto,%20Japan&ftid=0x600108942d033ff7:0x4fe6128b929fa857&entry=gps&shh=CAE&lucs=,94297699,94231188,94280568,47071704,94218641,94282134,100813469,94286869&g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Okonomiyaki Mr Young Men</a> – Traditional Kyoto-style okonomiyaki</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/bZN9oyrC2DNDGeFM8?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Omitsu</a> – Okonomiyaki</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/RWotoVFr6fE9ZUTR6?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Kyoto Tonkatsu Katsuda Shijokarasuma</a> – Tonkatsu</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/eu7YduGd2nNnJdyZ7?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>GYUKATSU Kyoto Katsugyu Kyoto Ekimae</a> – Gyukatsu</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/GQxMz4JzdRMXD4W19?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>GYUMON Nishiki Market</a> – Halal Wagyu & Vegan Ramen</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/xz3SAujhmhsmP5F57?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Gyumabushi Miyama</a> – Wagyu beef</li>
      <li class="prose-body"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/3GPN8ARptwtZjudF8?g_st=ic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Arashiyama Miyoshitei</a> – Wagyu dining in Arashiyama</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>FAQ</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">How to Get to Kyoto</h4>
    <p>Kyoto is easy to reach from most major cities in Japan. If you're travelling from Tokyo, the fastest option is the Shinkansen (bullet train), which takes around 2 hours. You can also reach Kyoto by train from nearby cities like Osaka and Nara, making it an excellent base for exploring the Kansai region.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Is 3 Days Enough in Kyoto?</h4>
    <p>While I personally spent five days in the city, I believe three well-planned days are enough to experience Kyoto's highlights without turning your trip into a race from one attraction to the next.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Is Kyoto Worth Visiting on Your First Trip to Japan?</h4>
    <p>Absolutely.</p>
    <p>Between its historic temples, traditional streets, bamboo forests and nearby day trips like Nara, Kyoto offers a completely different side of Japan from cities like Tokyo or Osaka. If it's your first trip to Japan, I think it's a destination you shouldn't miss.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">How to Get Around Kyoto?</h4>
    <p>Kyoto is relatively easy to get around, but it's also much larger than many first-time visitors expect. I think a lot of the frustration comes from trying to fit too many attractions into a single day rather than from the transport system itself. Grouping nearby sights together, like in this itinerary, makes exploring the city much easier and more enjoyable.</p>
    <p>During my trip, I used a Suica card on trains, buses and the subway, and found Google Maps surprisingly accurate for planning routes. One thing to keep in mind, however, is that buses can get caught in traffic, especially during busy periods. Whenever possible, I found trains to be the faster and more reliable option.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">What Time Should You Visit Arashiyama Bamboo Grove?</h4>
    <p>As early as possible.</p>
    <p>I visited shortly after sunrise, and it ended up being one of my favourite experiences in Japan. The grove becomes much busier later in the morning, so arriving early gives you the best chance to enjoy it before the crowds arrive.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Is Nara Worth a Day Trip from Kyoto?</h4>
    <p>Absolutely. If you're visiting Japan for the first time, I think Nara is one of the best day trips you can take from Kyoto. Between the free-roaming deer, Todai-ji Temple and the relaxed atmosphere around Nara Park, it offers a completely different experience from Kyoto itself.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Final thoughts">
      <p>If I could give you one piece of advice, it would be not to try to see everything. Some of my favourite memories from Kyoto came from the moments between the famous landmarks—watching the sunrise in Arashiyama, wandering through the streets of Higashiyama and sitting on the terrace at Yusa-tei while the rain fell outside.</p>
      <p>Before your trip, don't forget to save <a href="#section-google-map" class={mapLink}>the interactive map</a> so you can easily follow this itinerary while you're in Kyoto. Have an amazing trip, and I hope Kyoto leaves you wanting to come back, just as it did for me.</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>
  </div>
</section>
